{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ab60113763","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-329","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Brazil. It was first surveyed in 1977.</li><li>Its surface area is 384 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Eastwick is in northern Chile. It was first surveyed in 1925.</li><li>Its surface area is 205 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Sable and Lake Eastwick.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Lake Sable and Lake Eastwick are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1977, while Lake Eastwick was first surveyed in 1925.","The surface area of Lake Sable (384 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Eastwick (205 square kilometers).","Lake Sable is in southern Brazil, and Lake Eastwick is in northern Chile."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f67f1442ee","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-330","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Juniper Tunnel is in northern India. It opened in 1908.</li><li>Its length is 53 kilometers.</li><li>The Wexford Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1966.</li><li>Its length is 39 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Juniper Tunnel and the Wexford Tunnel.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Juniper Tunnel opened in 1908, while the Wexford Tunnel opened in 1966.","Both the Juniper Tunnel and the Wexford Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The length of the Juniper Tunnel (53 kilometers) is greater than that of the Wexford Tunnel (39 kilometers).","The Juniper Tunnel is in northern India, and the Wexford Tunnel is in southern Brazil."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.827003b80b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-330","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Juniper Tunnel is in northern India. It opened in 1908.</li><li>Its length is 53 kilometers.</li><li>The Wexford Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 1966.</li><li>Its length is 39 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the length of the two tunnels differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Juniper Tunnel (53 kilometers) is greater than that of the Wexford Tunnel (39 kilometers).","Both the Juniper Tunnel and the Wexford Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Juniper Tunnel, which is in northern India, is one of several tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Juniper Tunnel opened in 1908, while the Wexford Tunnel opened in 1966."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the length of both tunnels. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tunnel."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a8b69190f5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-332","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Ironwood Bridge is in central Canada. It opened in 1927.</li><li>Its main span is 1,390 meters.</li><li>The Wexford Bridge is in western Scotland. It opened in 1990.</li><li>Its main span is 873 meters.</li><li>Both are cable-stayed bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two bridges opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Ironwood Bridge opened in 1927, while the Wexford Bridge opened in 1990.","The main span of the Ironwood Bridge (1,390 meters) is greater than that of the Wexford Bridge (873 meters).","The Ironwood Bridge is in central Canada, and the Wexford Bridge is in western Scotland.","Both the Ironwood Bridge and the Wexford Bridge are cable-stayed bridges."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2de98dcdcb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-341","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Northam Tunnel is in western Scotland. It opened in 1924.</li><li>Its length is 45 kilometers.</li><li>The Halden Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 23 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Northam Tunnel (45 kilometers) is greater than that of the Halden Tunnel (23 kilometers).","The Northam Tunnel is in western Scotland, and the Halden Tunnel is in southern Norway.","Both the Northam Tunnel and the Halden Tunnel are railway tunnels.","The Northam Tunnel opened in 1924, while the Halden Tunnel opened in 1894."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2678a71dd3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-342","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Kingsley Museum is in southern New Zealand. It opened in 1959.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 266,405 visitors.</li><li>The Halden Museum is in eastern Australia. It opened in 2010.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 241,101 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two museums opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The annual attendance of the Kingsley Museum (266,405 visitors) is greater than that of the Halden Museum (241,101 visitors).","The Kingsley Museum opened in 1959, while the Halden Museum opened in 2010.","Both the Kingsley Museum and the Halden Museum are art museums with free admission.","The Kingsley Museum is in southern New Zealand, and the Halden Museum is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a24c3035e6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-349","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kingsley National Park is in southern Brazil. It was established in 1920.</li><li>Its area is 8,587 square kilometers.</li><li>Thornbury National Park is in eastern Australia. It was established in 1907.</li><li>Its area is 7,401 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the area of the two parks differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kingsley National Park was established in 1920, while Thornbury National Park was established in 1907.","Kingsley National Park, which is in southern Brazil, is one of several parks known for their old-growth forests.","Both Kingsley National Park and Thornbury National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Kingsley National Park (8,587 square kilometers) is greater than that of Thornbury National Park (7,401 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the area of both parks. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one park."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7eecb2aefb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-351","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Eastwick National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1969.</li><li>Its area is 6,719 square kilometers.</li><li>Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia. It was established in 1962.</li><li>Its area is 6,346 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Eastwick National Park was established in 1969, while Wexford National Park was established in 1962.","Eastwick National Park is in central Canada, and Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia.","Both Eastwick National Park and Wexford National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Eastwick National Park (6,719 square kilometers) is greater than that of Wexford National Park (6,346 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a52687a6f3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-359","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1915.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>The Umber Observatory is in western Scotland. It began operating in 1899.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 6 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Sable Observatory and the Umber Observatory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Sable Observatory began operating in 1915, while the Umber Observatory began operating in 1899.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Sable Observatory (8 meters) is greater than that of the Umber Observatory (6 meters).","Both the Sable Observatory and the Umber Observatory are radio observatories.","The Sable Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Umber Observatory is in western Scotland."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0b5c2513ea","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-361","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Merrow Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1902.</li><li>Its length is 55 kilometers.</li><li>The Sable Tunnel is in southern Brazil. It opened in 2006.</li><li>Its length is 43 kilometers.</li><li>Both are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Merrow Tunnel and the Sable Tunnel are tunnels that pass under a mountain range.","The Merrow Tunnel opened in 1902, while the Sable Tunnel opened in 2006.","The Merrow Tunnel is in southern Norway, and the Sable Tunnel is in southern Brazil.","The length of the Merrow Tunnel (55 kilometers) is greater than that of the Sable Tunnel (43 kilometers)."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b8d0da4a22","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-370","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,274 meters.</li><li>Mount Sable is in southern Brazil. It was first climbed in 1999.</li><li>Its height is 2,694 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Fenwick (3,274 meters) is greater than that of Mount Sable (2,694 meters).","Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland, and Mount Sable is in southern Brazil.","Mount Fenwick was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Sable was first climbed in 1999.","Both Mount Fenwick and Mount Sable are dormant volcanoes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7f49ae19e3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-372","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Fenwick Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1983.</li><li>Its height is 176 meters.</li><li>The Cresthill Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 1933.</li><li>Its height is 148 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Fenwick Tower is in eastern Australia, and the Cresthill Tower is in central Spain.","The Fenwick Tower was completed in 1983, while the Cresthill Tower was completed in 1933.","The height of the Fenwick Tower (176 meters) is greater than that of the Cresthill Tower (148 meters).","Both the Fenwick Tower and the Cresthill Tower are observation towers open to the public."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.edcbd669d1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Tower is in central Canada, and the Northam Tower is in northern India.","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2e07b8de14","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-386","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Bridge is in western Kenya. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its main span is 2,037 meters.</li><li>The Glenrock Bridge is in northern India. It opened in 1961.</li><li>Its main span is 1,342 meters.</li><li>Both are cable-stayed bridges.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Pembrook Bridge and the Glenrock Bridge.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Pembrook Bridge and the Glenrock Bridge are cable-stayed bridges.","The Pembrook Bridge is in western Kenya, and the Glenrock Bridge is in northern India.","The main span of the Pembrook Bridge (2,037 meters) is greater than that of the Glenrock Bridge (1,342 meters).","The Pembrook Bridge opened in 1894, while the Glenrock Bridge opened in 1961."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8545e7b0d1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-393","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 422 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Northam is in eastern Australia. It was first surveyed in 2003.</li><li>Its surface area is 363 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Redfern, which is in eastern Poland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Redfern was first surveyed in 1912, while Lake Northam was first surveyed in 2003.","Both Lake Redfern and Lake Northam are reservoirs created by dams.","The surface area of Lake Redfern (422 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Northam (363 square kilometers)."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3899bdc4ed","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-393","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 422 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Northam is in eastern Australia. It was first surveyed in 2003.</li><li>Its surface area is 363 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two lakes were first surveyed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Redfern (422 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Northam (363 square kilometers).","Lake Redfern was first surveyed in 1912, while Lake Northam was first surveyed in 2003.","Both Lake Redfern and Lake Northam are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Redfern is in eastern Poland, and Lake Northam is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1d645325bb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Sable and Lake Valewood.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers).","Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","Both Lake Sable and Lake Valewood are reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Sable is in southern Norway, and Lake Valewood is in northern India."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.340ac5c807","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-396","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Sable is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1904.</li><li>Its surface area is 663 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Valewood is in northern India. It was first surveyed in 2007.</li><li>Its surface area is 313 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Sable was first surveyed in 1904, while Lake Valewood was first surveyed in 2007.","The surface area of Lake Sable (663 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Valewood (313 square kilometers).","Lake Sable, which is in southern Norway, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Both Lake Sable and Lake Valewood are reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eaecb6c507","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-5","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Duarte is a marine biologist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Duarte recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Duarte found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Duarte concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.","Duarte concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","To investigate the question, Duarte recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Marine biologist Omar Duarte set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0a255eb78b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-11","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Mbeki is an ecologist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Mbeki compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Mbeki found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Mbeki compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Mbeki found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Mbeki concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Ecologist Omar Mbeki set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.14fe2c7eff","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-20","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Nakamura is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","Agricultural scientist Dmitri Nakamura set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","To investigate the question, Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.18bafb49e4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-28","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ayumi Okafor is a materials scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Materials scientist Ayumi Okafor set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.","Okafor found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","To investigate the question, Okafor coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a3335c036b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-37","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Castellano is a sociologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Castellano surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Castellano found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Castellano concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","Sociologist Dmitri Castellano set out to learn whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.","Castellano concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","To investigate the question, Castellano surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f4f7e725db","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-42","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Kowalski is a geologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Kowalski measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Kowalski found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Geologist Chen Kowalski set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.","Kowalski concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","To investigate the question, Kowalski measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d6e4bef794","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-54","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Zoologist Kenji Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.59cf145362","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-55","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Castellano is a zoologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Castellano gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Castellano found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Castellano concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Castellano gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Castellano concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Castellano found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.","Zoologist Kofi Castellano set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1a64d6d167","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-62","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Halvorsen is an astronomer.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Astronomer Beatriz Halvorsen set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Halvorsen concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e1063f691d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-63","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maren Brennan is an astronomer.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Astronomer Maren Brennan set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","To investigate the question, Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ee8920ac3b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-70","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Takeda is a linguist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Takeda concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Linguist Chen Takeda set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Takeda found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ad721ebb11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-71","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Delacroix is a linguist.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Linguist Leila Delacroix set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","Delacroix concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Delacroix found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","To investigate the question, Delacroix gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3793938c15","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-77","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Duarte is an economist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","Economist Nadia Duarte set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","To investigate the question, Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ae3b4bb555","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-77","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Duarte is an economist.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Economist Nadia Duarte set out to learn whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.","Duarte concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Duarte found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","To investigate the question, Duarte tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1edcd2ef6a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-81","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Mensah is a botanist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Mensah grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Mensah found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Mensah concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mensah found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Mensah concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.","Botanist Viktor Mensah set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","To investigate the question, Mensah grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8a5e36adef","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-84","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Rahman is an urban planner.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Rahman measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Rahman found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Rahman concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Rahman concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Rahman found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Rahman measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Urban planner Kofi Rahman set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e4b3a2ebbf","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-94","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Halvorsen is a hydrologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Samuel Halvorsen set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.539fd9b4c7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-100","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an education researcher.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Education researcher Lucía Sato set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.","Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","To investigate the question, Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1adead8001","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-100","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an education researcher.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Sato found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.","To investigate the question, Sato had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Education researcher Lucía Sato set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.36eabc85ac","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-111","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Takeda is a primatologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.</li><li>Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.</li><li>Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.</li><li>Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda found that young chimpanzees in the nut-cracking groups began using stones much earlier.","To investigate the question, Takeda observed young chimpanzees in groups where adults cracked nuts with stones and in groups where adults did not.","Primatologist Tomás Takeda set out to learn whether young chimpanzees learn to use tools by watching adults.","Takeda concluded that young chimpanzees may learn tool use by observation."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b1105c096d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-132","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Okafor is an archaeologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Archaeologist Amara Okafor set out to learn whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.","To investigate the question, Okafor analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.","Okafor found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fe9a184890","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-145","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Priya Takeda is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Marine ecologist Priya Takeda set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Takeda found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Takeda concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Takeda fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4eb03bb05b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-147","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Haddad is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","To investigate the question, Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Marine ecologist Amara Haddad set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2db223c9e8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-155","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Okafor is a climate scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Climate scientist Ravi Okafor set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f52dddbc34","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-173","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maren Iyer is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Iyer added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Iyer found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Iyer concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Iyer found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Forest ecologist Maren Iyer set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients.","Iyer concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Iyer added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.94cdfac9d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-177","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Vance is a music researcher.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Music researcher Zara Vance set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","To investigate the question, Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.11f4a7f121","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-179","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Delacroix is a music researcher.</li><li>Delacroix wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Delacroix found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","Delacroix concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Music researcher Beatriz Delacroix set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","To investigate the question, Delacroix tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
